L["adulteress"] = """Early in the morning, Jesus appeared again in the temple courts, where all the people gathered around him, and he sat down to teach them. The teachers of the law and the Pharisees brought in a woman caught in adultery. They made her stand before the group. The scene was meant to humiliate her, but it was really a trap for Jesus.

Teacher, they said to Jesus, this woman was caught in the act of adultery. In the Law Moses commanded us to stone such women. Now what do you say? If Jesus said to let her go, they could accuse him of ignoring the law of Moses. If he said to carry out the penalty, he would lose his reputation for mercy and could be in trouble with the Roman authorities, who did not allow the Jews to carry out executions.

But Jesus bent down and started to write on the ground with his finger. John doesn't tell us what he wrote. When they kept questioning him, he straightened up and said to them, let any one of you who is without sin be the first to throw a stone at her. Again he stooped down and wrote on the ground.

At this, those who heard began to go away one at a time, the older ones first, until only Jesus was left, with the woman still standing there. Jesus straightened up and asked her, woman, where are they? Has no one condemned you? No one, sir, she said. Then neither do I condemn you, Jesus declared. Go now and leave your life of sin.

Why does this matter? The accusers cared more about the trap than about the woman. They used her as a tool. Jesus saw her as a person. He didn't deny that sin is sin, and he told her to leave her life of sin. But he gave her mercy first, and then he gave her a way forward. Grace came before the instruction.

What happened after? The next thing Jesus said in John's gospel was, I am the light of the world. Whoever follows me will never walk in darkness, but will have the light of life. The woman went away without condemnation, and we are not told what became of her. But those who stood against her had a different problem. They had to face their own hearts.

What does this mean for us today? We are all quick to pick up stones, whether real ones or the stones of gossip, judgment and shame. Jesus' challenge still works. Before condemning someone else, we need to check our own record. And it also offers hope to those who know they have failed. The only one without sin, the only one who had the right to condemn her, chose to forgive. He did not say that what she did was fine. He said that she was free to start again.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus meets sinners with mercy and truth together, and that he gives people a real chance to begin again. And it points to the cross, where Jesus took the condemnation that we deserve."""
